WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.445 --> 00:00:03.703
By 2050, there are going to be

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:06.273
almost 10 billion of us
living on this planet.

00:00:07.785 --> 00:00:10.860
And all of us will need food to survive.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.080
If we don't change
anything and we will go on

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.360
in the way we are now consuming our food,

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.030
then the next 40 years,

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.640
we need to produce as much food as we did

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.840
over the last 8,000 years.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.083
And that's a huge challenge.

00:00:28.252 --> 00:00:30.170
Agriculture is the largest source

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.078
of non-CO2 greenhouse gas emissions.

00:00:33.078 --> 00:00:36.380
And, along with climate
change and urbanization,

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.700
the resources that we
need to feed everyone,

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.580
things like space and water,

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.413
are becoming harder to find.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.270
That's why scientists and
farmers across the world

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.603
are finding new ways to help
us and our planet thrive.

00:00:58.563 --> 00:00:59.600
So you're now in the central area

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:00.940
of the Netherlands.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.940
And within this area we have
many different institutes

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.220
and industries that are focusing

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.013
on the big challenges in food.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.210
We like to study things in nature,

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.640
try to learn from things in nature,

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.100
and try to transfer this into solutions

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.073
that can improve the quality
of life of humankind.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.060
Wageningen University is known

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.630
as the Silicon Valley of agriculture.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.570
Students and researchers
from more than 100 countries

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:34.160
work here in vast greenhouses and labs

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.060
to determine the future of our
food and how we'll grow it.

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:41.480
The Green Revolution in the 20th century

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.787
started by a guy named Borlaug

00:01:43.787 --> 00:01:46.160
and he was a plant breeder.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.985
And what I like about the story

00:01:47.985 --> 00:01:51.770
is that the guy got the
Nobel Prize for Peace.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:53.210
Not for science, but for peace.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.610
And the reason was that he came up

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:57.787
with new cultivars of wheat

00:01:57.787 --> 00:02:01.100
that were able to produce more per plant.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.070
And, in that way, he
could offer a solution

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:07.610
for the hunger that was quite important

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.670
at that time in Asia and in Africa.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:12.950
The bad thing was, of course,
that these new cultivars

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.670
also needed a lot of fertilizers,

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:17.330
a lot of pesticides.

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.830
And that's the reason
why I think that we need

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.240
a new Green Revolution.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.210
We need to produce more per square meter,

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:24.210
but at the same time

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.960
we need to use less fertilizer,
less energy, less water.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.770
So, more with less and better

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:32.403
is really a summary of
our research programs.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.580
Along with the driverless tractors

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.520
and agroforestry being developed here

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.740
at the university's research farm,

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.370
the next Green Revolution
could rely on optimizing

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:47.163
the most fundamental process
in every plant, photosynthesis.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.780
All life depends, now, on photosynthesis.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:53.260
So, it's an essential
building block of life.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:54.093
For a long time,

00:02:54.093 --> 00:02:56.630
people thought this could
not be changed anymore.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:58.540
They thought this is a fixed thing.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.510
It already exist for
about 2 billion years,

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:01.447
so very long time,

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:02.870
and people thought the evolution

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:04.500
would have taken care of this.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.233
And now it looks like it's not.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.180
The team created a robot
filled with cameras

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.020
that can image plants
while they photosynthesize

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.513
using a property called
chlorophyll fluorescence.

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:20.670
By examining photosynthesis
at the DNA level,

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.120
they identified natural genetic variations

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.550
in the way plants handle light.

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:27.430
That data can be used to breed crops

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:30.173
which are almost 50%
better at the process.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:33.400
Not only could that double yields,

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.560
but the optimized plants
will be more efficient

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.580
with resources like water
and nutrients in the soil.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.214
So, what the Green Revolution taught us

00:03:40.214 --> 00:03:43.470
how by changing the
architecture of plants,

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.490
we could harvest more of the plant

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:49.760
and that's probably saved
humanity from a lot of trouble.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.520
Now, this new step in
looking at photosynthesis

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:53.730
could mean the same.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.390
So, if we can improve

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:57.980
with only a few
percentages photosynthesis,

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:01.620
we would also be able to
improve plant productivity.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:05.390
And if you can do that in a
sustainable and durable way

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:08.570
for a long time and making it
available to a lot of people,

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:11.240
by just increasing one trait,

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:14.113
then would give us a
tremendous boost forwards.

00:04:16.224 --> 00:04:18.310
On the other side of the world in China,

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:21.510
improving food production to
feed its 1.4 billion people

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.273
is already an urgent problem.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.610
This small-scale peasant economy

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.190
means lower production at a higher cost.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:57.570
And, combined with food safety

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:00.640
and sustainability challenges,
this creates major problems

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:02.590
for the country's agriculture industry.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.720
Dr. Li Shaohua is the research director

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.650
at Sanan Sino-Science,
a joint venture between

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:12.713
the Chinese Academy of
Science and a local LED maker.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.380
In 2016, they built this factory

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.450
made up of eight three-story buildings

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.603
to grow medicinal plants and vegetables.

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:25.150
This one building covers
just under an acre of land

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:28.347
but provides 1.8 tons of
produce every single day.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:47.070
Their secret?

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:48.330
A modular planting system

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.570
made up of one-meter square boards

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:54.030
which contain lighting and
storage for a nutrient solution.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:55.780
The boards easily slot together

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:57.880
and can be replaced quickly if they break.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:33.400
There's no need for pesticides

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.820
and the cooling system
recycles evaporated water

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:37.090
back to the plants

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:39.310
so that some use over 18 times less

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:41.093
than if they were grown in a field.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:45.740
For now, most of the vegetables are sold

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:48.890
to local supermarkets
across Fujian Province

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:51.430
and one bag of lettuce costs 11 times more

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:53.530
than others on the shelf.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:55.270
But the price is likely to get lower

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:57.533
as the technology becomes more widespread.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:24.670
Solving problems in food production

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:28.970
will also mean that you are
working on solving issues

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:31.360
that affects the whole Earth.

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:33.730
It's a combination of feeling responsible

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:34.750
for the environment

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:38.283
and feeling responsible for
feeding the growing population.

